1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,178
Asana's 2021 Anatomy of Work survey of more than

2
00:00:03,178 --> 00:00:06,753
ten thousand workers found that about sixty percent of

3
00:00:06,753 --> 00:00:09,070
their time went to work about work:

4
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:10,063
status threads,

5
00:00:10,063 --> 00:00:11,321
calendar shuffling,

6
00:00:11,321 --> 00:00:12,513
chasing approvals,

7
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:14,499
and searching for information.

8
00:00:14,499 --> 00:00:18,141
Millions of smart people finish Friday evening drained,

9
00:00:18,141 --> 00:00:21,252
yet struggle to point to one lasting thing they

10
00:00:21,252 --> 00:00:23,040
actually finished all week.

11
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:27,169
Georgetown computer science professor Cal Newport traces this burnout

12
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:30,699
epidemic to a structural flaw he calls Pseudo-Productivity.

13
00:00:30,699 --> 00:00:32,853
On twentieth-century factory floors,

14
00:00:32,853 --> 00:00:36,324
supervisors could count physical widgets coming off a belt

15
00:00:36,324 --> 00:00:36,982
every hour.

16
00:00:36,982 --> 00:00:40,991
When knowledge work arrived without an obvious hourly widget count,

17
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:45,120
workplaces lazily substituted visible activity as a proxy for effort.

18
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,693
Keeping a green chat dot lit for ten hours,

19
00:00:47,693 --> 00:00:49,787
answering emails in ninety seconds,

20
00:00:49,787 --> 00:00:53,856
and sitting in back-to-back meetings looks productive while starving

21
00:00:53,856 --> 00:00:56,010
every project that actually matters.

22
00:00:56,010 --> 00:00:59,631
Research explains why overloaded task lists collapse.

23
00:00:59,631 --> 00:01:03,389
Professor Sophie Leroy showed that when you switch away

24
00:01:03,389 --> 00:01:05,028
from an unfinished task,

25
00:01:05,028 --> 00:01:07,830
part of your attention stays stuck on it.

26
00:01:07,830 --> 00:01:10,152
She called this attention residue.

27
00:01:10,152 --> 00:01:10,904
Separately,

28
00:01:10,904 --> 00:01:15,277
Gloria Mark's research found it takes about twenty-three minutes

29
00:01:15,277 --> 00:01:18,419
on average to get back to an interrupted task.

30
00:01:18,419 --> 00:01:20,401
Juggle eight active projects,

31
00:01:20,401 --> 00:01:23,543
and each one sends its own stream of check-ins

32
00:01:23,543 --> 00:01:24,090
all day.

33
00:01:24,090 --> 00:01:28,692
Slow Productivity flips that broken math using two foundational shifts:

34
00:01:28,692 --> 00:01:29,729
Do Fewer Things,

35
00:01:29,729 --> 00:01:31,480
and Work at a Natural Pace.

36
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:34,656
Capping your major commitments at two or three at

37
00:01:34,656 --> 00:01:37,703
a time cuts the check-ins and handoffs that eat

38
00:01:37,703 --> 00:01:38,351
your week,

39
00:01:38,351 --> 00:01:40,879
freeing energy to actually finish work.

40
00:01:40,879 --> 00:01:44,833
Working at a natural pace borrows from physiologist Nathaniel

41
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:48,333
Kleitman's idea of ninety-minute rest-activity cycles:

42
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:50,926
pair focused sprints with real recovery,

43
00:01:50,926 --> 00:01:55,010
and pace big goals across calm seasons instead of all-nighters.

44
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:58,549
Newport's third principle locks the entire system together:

45
00:01:58,549 --> 00:01:59,748
Obsess Over Quality.

46
00:01:59,748 --> 00:02:02,268
In both careers and solo media businesses,

47
00:02:02,268 --> 00:02:03,467
shipping ten rushed,

48
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:06,646
average deliverables generates zero lasting leverage,

49
00:02:06,646 --> 00:02:11,385
whereas producing one undeniably thoughtful flagship asset compounds reputation

50
00:02:11,385 --> 00:02:12,644
and demand for years.

51
00:02:12,644 --> 00:02:13,304
Better yet,

52
00:02:13,304 --> 00:02:16,723
signature quality earns you immunity from office theater,

53
00:02:16,723 --> 00:02:20,562
colleagues and audiences gladly forgive a slower afternoon email

54
00:02:20,562 --> 00:02:23,921
reply when the finished work you deliver solves problems

55
00:02:23,921 --> 00:02:25,900
at a level nobody else can touch.

56
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:30,796
Deploy this operating system tomorrow morning using three concrete rules.

57
00:02:30,796 --> 00:02:31,198
First,

58
00:02:31,198 --> 00:02:35,223
replace your endless to-do list with a Two-List Pull System:

59
00:02:35,223 --> 00:02:38,241
park incoming requests inside a Holding Tank,

60
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:41,192
and enforce a strict limit of three items on

61
00:02:41,192 --> 00:02:42,332
your Active List,

62
00:02:42,332 --> 00:02:46,222
nothing new gets pulled in until one active project ships.

63
00:02:46,222 --> 00:02:46,691
Second,

64
00:02:46,691 --> 00:02:51,185
protect one sealed ninety-minute Ultradian Deep Block every morning

65
00:02:51,185 --> 00:02:54,740
with your phone in another room before opening email.

66
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:55,142
Third,

67
00:02:55,142 --> 00:02:56,416
batch all messages,

68
00:02:56,416 --> 00:02:57,087
logistics,

69
00:02:57,087 --> 00:03:01,178
and admin into two crisp twenty-five-minute windows at midday

70
00:03:01,178 --> 00:03:02,050
and four P.M.
